# Issues #947, #879 and #980, the negative direction. `spec/error-model.md:165-169` is a
# delimiter-agnostic MUST NOT: *"on any recovery path, for any malformed input, a parser MUST NOT
# raise any unmatched-delimiter diagnostic — the class whose members in v0.1 are
# `ol-unmatched-paren`, `ol-unmatched-brace`, and `ol-unmatched-bracket` — for a delimiter that is,
# in fact, correctly matched in the source."*
#
# Every program below is malformed, and every delimiter in it is correctly matched. So none may
# raise any member of that class; `ol-bad-token` names the real defect instead. Before the fix the
# paren rows raised `ol-unmatched-paren` twice each on balanced parens (#879), and the bracket and
# brace rows raised their own class members on balanced delimiters.
#
# This fixture is one half of a pair. Its partner,
# `heritage/check/a-genuinely-unmatched-delimiter-is-still-reported`, pins that a delimiter which
# really has no partner IS still reported — without which a parser could satisfy this file by
# never emitting the class at all.
#
# A reserved word cannot be a `callable-name` (`spec/grammar.md:211`), which is what makes each
# paren row malformed. The rows use Data and Heritage names, so both profiles are active.
print ( set 1 )
print ( if 1 )
print ( import 1 )
print ( in 1 )
print ( struct 1 )
print ( add 1 )
print ( value )

# A missing operand, in each of the three delimiters.
print ( 1 + )
print [ 1 + ]
print { a: 1 + }

# A selector with no key-term between brackets that are one character apart.
print :nums[]

# Nested: an inner bracket defect inside an outer group, both balanced.
print ( [ 1 + ] )
